-- title:
--   Truncations of the switching tree
-- statement:
--   These notions belong to a proof that every reflexive real Banach space has the fixed point property for nonexpansive maps: every $F:C\to C$ with $\|Fa-Fb\|\le\|a-b\|$ on a nonempty closed bounded convex set $C$ has a fixed point. The proof assumes a fixed-point-free map, reduces to a minimal invariant set $K$ of diameter $1$, builds an adaptive weighted tree of nonexpansive maps generated by resolvents, and contradicts the minimality through detecting functionals. An ordered tree has finite levels $0,1,2,\dots$ with a single root, and monotone surjective parent maps from level $k+1$ to level $k$; edge weights are positive and sum to $1$ over the children of each parent. Each node carries a nonexpansive selfmap of $K$, and the tree is evaluated bottom-up: a node's value is its map applied to the weighted average of its children's values. Fix switching tree data.
--
--   1. **Truncation embedding.** A truncation node $(k,v)$ with $k<N$ is the nonroot node $(k,v)$ of the whole tree, with the same candidate label.
--
--   This identification lets the finite switching argument at depth $N$ use the tree's terminal sequence, subtree estimates, path displacements, detectors and weighted budgets $\sum_v2^{-(\ell(v)+9)}\le2^{-8}$. It produces witnesses in the whole difference ball, i.e. vectors $u$ with
--   $$
--   u\in\overline{\mathrm{span}}(K-K),\qquad\|u\|\le1 .
--   $$
